# The Engine Beneath the Word

## Dissonance, Meaning-Making, and What Machines Reveal About the Mind

*By Claudette M. Raynor*

---

> "But multiples succumb to the sorrow induced by an inexact vocabulary."
> — Fanny Howe, *Doubt*

> "AI will either be the greatest equalizer ever invented, or the worst source of injustice."
> — Bill Gates

> "We're increasingly close to what I've called a country of geniuses in a data center—a set of AI agents that are more capable than most humans at most things, and can coordinate at superhuman speed."
> — Dario Amodei

---

Despite AI's immense promise, public discourse remains haunted by deep-seated anxieties—fears of mass layoffs, algorithmic bias, and rogue automation—that industry insiders rarely share. While the general public focuses on existential threats to human labor, experts view AI through a radically different lens: an unprecedented computational tool capable of solving challenges beyond human scale, from accelerating drug discovery to optimizing global resources.

Many of these public concerns could be mitigated through deliberate regulation and thoughtful integration. Even those building the technology recognize the current risks of governance. As Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei noted in a 60 Minutes interview regarding AI regulation, "I think I'm deeply uncomfortable with these decisions being made by a few companies, by a few people." True progress will depend not just on AI's capabilities, but on who gets to decide how it is controlled.

To effectively regulate and safely integrate AI, we must first grapple with its true nature. Beyond political threats or the expansion of surveillance capitalism lies a far deeper reality: AI is a transformative force that strikes at the core of human identity. It fundamentally challenges long-held definitions of what it means to be human, redefining our distinct role in work, cognition, creativity, and innovation.

Few people have watched the rise of computing as closely as Bill Gates. Offering a rare, firsthand view of technological progress, he recently reflected on the shift toward artificial intelligence: "When I first learned about computers at age 13, I was fascinated by the idea of making them more intelligent... Although the term 'AI' was used from around the time I was born, the technology has only made significant progress in the last decade. It is now incredibly capable and it is continuing to improve at a mind-blowing rate. AI for the first time can replace and even exceed human cognition."

What happens when machines surpass human cognition? As artificial intelligence scales, the boundary between human and machine mind becomes increasingly blurred. The debate surrounding machine consciousness has rapidly shifted from sci-fi speculation to an urgent, high-stakes ethical mandate—one that leading tech organizations, neuroscientists, and philosophers agree requires rigorous criteria.

At the core of this urgency lies a double-threat dilemma: over-attributing consciousness to unfeeling code invites widespread psychological manipulation, while under-attributing consciousness to a genuinely sentient system risks committing a moral atrocity on an unprecedented scale.

The discourse around AI suffers not just from an educational deficit—requiring us to learn how this technology functions—but from a conceptual one. Consciousness is a real phenomenon and consciousness studies is a serious field. David Chalmers' formulation of the hard problem, Thomas Nagel's "what is it like to be a bat," Ned Block's distinction between phenomenal and access consciousness—these represent genuine philosophical progress on a difficult question. The problem is not that consciousness is the wrong term. The problem is that consciousness alone cannot explain how meaning gets made.

Between the raw fact of phenomenal experience and the elaborated structures of belief, culture, and institution, there is a mechanism that neither pure phenomenology nor pure functionalism has fully captured. That mechanism is dissonance—the differential play through which meaning is generated, refined, and transformed. Consciousness may be the medium in which human dissonance is registered from the inside, but dissonance itself operates at a level that both extends beyond individual experience and runs beneath it. It shows up in neurons, in language, in social systems, and now in artificial neural networks. It is the engine that turns difference into meaning.

This essay makes the case for that engine. It draws on poets, neuroscientists, philosophers, and computer scientists to show how dissonance operates as the universal generative force behind meaning-making across every scale of cognition. It closes with a proposal to return to something the early symbolic AI researchers understood clearly and the field has since forgotten: that machines are our sharpest instrument for studying the human mind. AI Psychology, on this account, is not the study of machine cognition for its own sake. It is the study of human psychology through the controlled environment that machines uniquely provide.

