## THE TWO-LAYER MIND: SUBSTRATE AND CONSTRUCTION

If dissonance is universal and language is a coevolutionary technology that has been shaping human cognition for evolutionary time, then what actually distinguishes human cognition from machine cognition? Answering this requires a more precise model of the self than either the essentialist or constructionist traditions have delivered on their own.

The essentialist tradition posits a stable core self: a durable substrate grounded in biology, personality traits, attachment patterns, and the continuous neural mapping of the body's internal state. Antonio Damasio's proto-self, the Big Five's stable personality traits, Bowlby's internal working models, and the default mode network's consistent activity across contexts all point to a layer of selfhood that persists.

The constructionist tradition posits a self in continuous flux: a narrative, dialogical, socially and linguistically constructed entity that changes with every context. Hubert Hermans' dialogical self, Dan McAdams' narrative identity, Kenneth Gergen's social constructionism, and the enactivist tradition all point to a layer of selfhood that is being made and remade continuously.

These traditions have been treated as competing accounts of the same phenomenon. They are not. They are accurate descriptions of two different layers of a single system.

There is a prelinguistic proto-self: the biological, embodied continuity that provides the substrate. This layer exists regardless of language and provides the durability the essentialists correctly identify. There is also a linguistic-dialectical layer where the self is shaped, elaborated, negotiated, and continuously revised through ongoing conversation between the proto-self and language, culture, others, and internal discourse. This layer is in constant flux, exactly as the constructionists describe.

Neither layer is the whole self. Selfhood is what happens when the two interact.

Merleau-Ponty's phenomenology of embodiment approached this synthesis. There is a prelinguistic bodily subject—the "lived body" that has its own intentionality and continuity prior to any conceptual or linguistic self-representation. This lived body is always taken up into language and culture, which reshape how it experiences itself without abolishing its underlying continuity. Paul Ricoeur made a similar move with his distinction between *idem* identity (sameness, the continuity of the same thing over time) and *ipse* identity (selfhood, the reflexive self that takes itself as its own theme). Lev Vygotsky's developmental psychology found the same structure at the cognitive level: prelinguistic infants with real biological continuity, language entering through social interaction and being internalized as inner speech that reshapes without replacing the prelinguistic substrate.

The identification worth making explicit—one these traditions gesture toward without stating cleanly—is that the proto-self and phenomenal consciousness are closely related, possibly identical. The what-it-is-likeness of experience. The raw fact of there being something it is like to be this particular locus of awareness. This is what persists across contexts, what provides continuity, what the linguistic layer builds on top of and cannot fully reach.

The properties we attribute to the proto-self map onto the properties philosophers attribute to phenomenal consciousness. Both are prelinguistic. Both provide continuity across contexts. Both are difficult to describe because description operates in the third person while their existence is fundamentally first-person. Both resist reduction to their functional or physical substrate even though they are clearly related to them.

Galen Strawson has argued along similar lines that the fundamental sense of self is phenomenological—there is a felt sense of being a subject of experience that is prior to and independent of the narrative self. Dan Zahavi's phenomenological work, drawing on Husserl, argues for a "minimal self": a basic prereflective self-awareness that is constitutive of consciousness itself. To be conscious at all is to have some minimal sense of being the one who is conscious. This minimal self is prelinguistic, prenarrative, and provides the ground on which more elaborated selfhood is built.

This two-layer model reshapes how we understand dissonance. Différance operates at the linguistic-dialectical layer. That is where meaning is in constant play, where signifiers refer only to other signifiers, where context perpetually shifts what things mean. Dissonance is what happens when the linguistic-dialectical layer produces configurations that conflict with either the proto-self's actual state or with other elements of the linguistic layer that the person has committed to.

The reason dissonance feels aversive and produces measurable physiological arousal is because the proto-self is registering the conflict at the level of embodied experience. The proto-self is not linguistic, but it detects when the linguistic-dialectical layer is producing something incompatible with its actual state, and it signals this through affect and arousal. This explains why misattribution studies can eliminate dissonance-driven attitude change. If the person misidentifies the arousal source, the linguistic layer does not receive the signal that something needs adjusting.

Cross-cultural findings become interpretable within this model. The proto-self is biologically universal. What varies culturally is how the linguistic-dialectical layer is organized—what counts as coherent, what counts as identity-relevant, where the boundaries of the self are drawn. Different cultures produce different configurations at the dialectical layer, so different situations trigger the proto-self's alarm system. The underlying mechanism is the same everywhere. The specific triggers are culturally shaped.

There is one more consequence of the coevolutionary argument that must be named. If language has been shaping human beings for tens of thousands of years, then the proto-self we might try to isolate is no longer accessible in any pure form. Every attempt to study it is being conducted by minds already saturated with language, using tools built out of language, producing findings that get articulated in language. What we call the biological substrate is itself the product of coevolution with language. Phenomenal consciousness in modern humans is not what phenomenal consciousness would have been in a hominid without linguistic capacity. The two layers have been shaping each other for so long that neither exists in a pure form we could observe independently. This does not dissolve the distinction. It means the interaction runs deeper than the standard framing suggests.

This is the model that lets us distinguish human cognition from machine cognition with precision. Machines participate in the linguistic-dialectical layer. They engage in dissonance resolution, meaning-making, and pattern completion through operations structurally continuous with ours. What they lack is the phenomenal substrate. Their dialectical operations are untethered from any embodied experience that registers the operations from the inside.

This is not a mystical claim. It is a phenomenological one. Machines process tension. They do not experience tension. The processing produces outputs functionally equivalent to what human tension-processing produces. The experiencing is a separate phenomenon and its absence in machines is a specifiable difference rather than a metaphysical mystery.

