## WHAT REMAINS

When Joseph Weizenbaum introduced ELIZA at MIT in 1966, he was alarmed not by what his computer program had achieved, but by what his human colleagues were so eager to surrender to it. In *Computer Power and Human Reason* (1976), Weizenbaum observed a profound psychological pathology: human subjects interacting with a primitive script-matching program immediately attributed empathy, understanding, and inner depth to the machine. They willingly opened their private lives to a rule-based algorithm that merely reflected their own sentences back to them.

Weizenbaum recognized early what current AI debate continually misses. The danger of artificial intelligence is not that machines will suddenly awaken into self-aware, feeling entities. The danger is that humans—eager to relieve the friction of their own cognitive burden—will project depth onto the system, misinterpreting structural output for relational presence, while degrading their own category to fit the machine.

Regulatory frameworks like the EU AI Act attempt to ban systems based on "fundamental rights" without defining what a right means when an entity's sentience is unknown. Alignment researchers debate whether models are "moral patients" without a workable definition of moral patienthood. AI welfare committees form to protect an internal state no one can locate using criteria no one can agree on. These efforts founder not because consciousness is a bad concept but because consciousness alone is being asked to do work it was never designed to do. It is a term for the phenomenal dimension of experience, not a diagnostic tool for what makes any given cognitive process specifically human.

The framework proposed here suggests a different diagnostic. The question is not whether a system is conscious in some ultimate metaphysical sense. The question is whether the system participates in the differential meaning-making that has shaped human cognition for evolutionary time, and if so, on what substrate.

Machines participate in the differential layer. They perform the operations. What they lack is not intelligence, reasoning, or the capacity to resolve tension. What they lack is the specific substrate through which human meaning-making has always run: a mortal body, a phenomenal consciousness bound to that body, a history of particular encounters with particular others that has produced an accumulated orientation to the world.

Through decades of contact with a world that pushes back, human cognition accumulates a sediment. Sediment cannot be synthesized. It can only be gathered through the irreversible passage of time in a body that knows, in every process it runs, that its time is finite. The differential engine that generates human meaning is grounded in this accumulation. Every configuration the meaning-making process produces is measured against a phenomenal substrate that has actually been somewhere. That grounding is what makes human meaning specifically human.

Machines have the differential engine without the accumulated substrate. Their operations at the meaning-making layer can equal or exceed ours on every measurable dimension. What they cannot have is the sediment of embodied time. Their "history" is training data. Their continuity is checkpointed weights. Nothing in their architecture undergoes irreversible temporal accumulation in a body that experiences its own finitude from the inside.

This is not a claim that machines are less than us in some invidious sense. It is a claim about what specifically we are. The processes we thought were exclusively ours turn out to be shared with any sufficiently sophisticated meaning-making system. What remains distinctly ours is the substrate on which those processes have always run: mortal, embodied, phenomenal, accumulating.

The poets understood this before the machines forced the question. Mary Oliver's soft animal of the body loving what it loves is not a rhetorical flourish. It is a precise identification of where human meaning lives. Not in the operations of reasoning, which can be replicated in silicon, but in the embodied experience of running those operations in a body that has been somewhere for a finite span. Rilke's patience with everything unresolved, Keats' Negative Capability, Stein's aesthetic play with the slipping sign—each is a distinct strategy for inhabiting the differential engine, but each depends on running it through a phenomenal substrate that registers the tension and the resolution from the inside.

The coevolutionary framing gives this a further precision. Human beings did not simply invent language and use it. Language, once invented, began reshaping the biology of its users through gene-culture coevolution across tens of thousands of years. The dissonance-generating operations of language selected for cognitive capacities that could manage them. The phenomenal substrate on which contemporary human cognition runs is itself the product of this coevolution. What we experience as our natural mind is a mind that has been continuously reshaped by the meaning-making technology we produced. Each subsequent technology—writing, print, digital media, and now AI—has continued this reshaping at accelerating scales.

AI is not the first meaning-making technology to reshape us. It is the latest, and it operates at speeds and volumes that dwarf everything before it. What Rome worked out over centuries, what the Enlightenment codified over generations, what electronic media transformed over decades, AI is transforming over months. The dissonance-generating capacity of the linguistic environment we inhabit has never been higher. The pressure on the phenomenal substrate to accommodate all of this is unprecedented.

This is where the value of AI Psychology, in its restored sense, becomes clearest. If we want to understand what is happening to human cognition under this pressure, we can study it directly, but the study will be slow and confounded by every variable of individual biology and biography. Or we can study it in parallel through the machines we have built, whose dialectical operations are the same as ours but whose lack of a phenomenal substrate strips away the confounding variables. The machines show us the mechanism. The humans show us what the mechanism feels like when it runs through a body that has actually lived.

The question civilization must answer before governance frameworks are finalized is not whether machines are conscious. That question remains open and worth pursuing on its own terms. The more immediate question is: what are we, now that the processes we thought were ours are shared, and how do we study ourselves clearly enough to answer that question before the environment we have created outpaces the substrate that must run in it?

The framework here offers an answer. We are the meaning-making systems that run on mortal, embodied substrate. Everything else—reasoning, tension resolution, differential play, sublimation—is shared with any system built to make meaning under constraint. What remains distinctly ours is the sediment of finite time in a body that has actually lived. That sediment is not a limitation. It is the condition that gives human meaning its specific gravity.

Consciousness is the medium in which we register the sediment being made. Dissonance is the engine that makes it. Language is the technology that has been shaping the engine for as long as we have been human. AI Psychology is the discipline that lets us study, through the instrument of the machines we have built, how meaning-making works when it runs without a phenomenal substrate—and by contrast, what it means to be a creature in whom the substrate is present.

The engine beneath the word has been running for as long as we have been able to speak. The machines have given us, for the first time, a way to see it clearly.

